# Environment template for deep-wiki.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` at the repository root before running anything:
#
#     cp env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored and holds your real values; this file is committed and
# must never contain a secret. Every variable declared in
# packages/contracts/src/env.ts has to appear here — `bun run check` fails if
# the two drift apart.

# Runtime mode. One of: development | production | test
NODE_ENV=development

# Port the API listens on.
# Keep this at 1024 or above: rootless podman cannot bind lower ports.
PORT=3000

# Postgres connection string. Must be a valid URL.
# Matches the credentials used by the compose stack, and its port must equal
# POSTGRES_HOST_PORT below — `bun run env:check` compares the two.
#
# 5432 stays here, and that is deliberate: this file is a template every
# developer copies, so its ports are the conventional ones even on a machine
# that cannot use them (docs/RUNNING.md §6, trap 4 — a port like 25432 in this
# file is somebody's laptop, and gets reverted rather than accommodated). Move
# yours in `.env`, which is gitignored and exists for exactly this.
#
# Know what that leaves open. On the machine this was written on, ports 5432,
# 1025, 8025, 9000 and 9001 all belong to an unrelated `menukap` compose
# project. `podman compose up` fails loudly on a collision, so the *stack*
# cannot start — but the app does not fail: this line names localhost:5432
# whatever happens to be listening there, so a developer who follows the
# template connects successfully to another project's Postgres. The symptom is
# not a refused connection. It is wrong or missing data, and that reads as a
# bug in deep-wiki; this project has already lost time to that class twice.
# No port check can catch it, because the port is open — it is just the wrong
# server answering. See docs/TODO.md, Findings 2026-09-09.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://deepwiki:deepwiki@localhost:5432/deepwiki

# Public base URL, used to build links embedded in email (invitations,
# password reset).
# The browser-facing origin of apps/web — NOT the API. Two things depend on it:
# it is the single origin the API allows through CORS with credentials, and it
# is the host of the /reset-password and /invite/accept links mailed to users.
#
# In development that is the Nuxt dev server. It is not PORT above, and it is
# not a number to guess: apps/web/nuxt.config.ts declares `devServer.port`, and
# this must name the same origin. `bun run env:check` compares the two.
#
# Get this wrong and the browser stops the sign-in request itself. The API
# names APP_URL as the one origin it allows through CORS with credentials; when
# that is not the origin the page was served from, the browser rejects the
# credentialed cross-origin exchange before the page ever sees a response.
# Measured 2026-09-09 against a live stack with this line deliberately
# mismatched: Chromium logs `net::ERR_FAILED`, Firefox `NS_ERROR_DOM_BAD_URI`,
# the `fetch` rejects, no cookie is stored, and the sign-in screen shows
# "Could not reach the server. Check your connection and try again."
#
# So it is not silent — but it is misleading, which is the actual trap. The
# screen blames the connection and the connection is fine; nothing in either
# server's log says CORS. Until 2026-09-09 this comment claimed the opposite —
# "nothing reports an error", "login returns 200" — and both halves were wrong:
# the request is refused before it can be answered, so there is no 200 to read.
# The same wrong story is still told in scripts/checks/env-consistency.ts
# (header and SESSION_SYMPTOM), docs/RUNNING.md §3 and apps/web/nuxt.config.ts.
#
# This line read 4173 until 2026-09-08, which is Vite's preview port and the
# e2e harness's main-checkout web port (packages/db/testing/worktree.ts), and
# was never where `nuxt dev` listens.
APP_URL=http://localhost:3001

# Where the browser should reach apps/api. Must point at PORT above.
# Nuxt maps NUXT_PUBLIC_* onto runtimeConfig.public automatically.
NUXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Session lifetime. Idle timeout slides on activity; absolute timeout is a
# hard cap regardless of activity.
SESSION_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MINUTES=30
SESSION_ABSOLUTE_TIMEOUT_DAYS=30

# Token time-to-live.
PASSWORD_RESET_TTL_MINUTES=30
INVITATION_TTL_DAYS=7

# The page soft lock. A lock is held while the holder's last heartbeat is
# within PAGE_LOCK_TTL_SECONDS; the client heartbeats every
# PAGE_LOCK_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS, well inside the TTL.
PAGE_LOCK_TTL_SECONDS=120
PAGE_LOCK_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS=20

# The implicit changeset grouping window, in minutes: a save joins the same
# author's still-open changeset in a book when it falls within this many
# minutes of that changeset's last activity; otherwise it starts a new one.
# This is the only place this number exists — there is no schema default.
CHANGESET_WINDOW_MINUTES=30

# SMTP configuration for the MailSender adapter. SMTP_HOST and MAIL_FROM
# are required — the application always needs to be able to send mail for
# invitations and password reset. The values below match the compose
# stack's Mailpit service (no authentication needed), so a fresh clone
# boots with zero configuration.
#
# SMTP_SECURE takes one of: true | 1 | false | 0 (case and surrounding space
# do not matter, and an empty assignment reads as false). Anything else is
# refused at boot rather than guessed at — until 2026-09-09 the schema read
# this line with `z.coerce.boolean()`, which is `Boolean(value)`, so the
# `false` below arrived as `true` and every fresh clone opened TLS against a
# Mailpit that does not speak it.
SMTP_HOST=localhost
SMTP_PORT=1025
SMTP_SECURE=false
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
MAIL_FROM=noreply@deep-wiki.local

# BlobStore adapter selection. One of: filesystem | s3
# filesystem is the default — a self-hoster on a single VPS will not run
# MinIO. Only BLOB_STORE_FS_ROOT is required for the filesystem driver.
BLOB_STORE_DRIVER=filesystem
BLOB_STORE_FS_ROOT=./.data/blobs

# The variables below are only required when BLOB_STORE_DRIVER=s3. They
# stay empty here — no secret ships in this template. The values that
# would work against the local compose MinIO service are:
#   BLOB_STORE_S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
#   BLOB_STORE_S3_REGION=us-east-1
#   BLOB_STORE_S3_BUCKET=deep-wiki
#   BLOB_STORE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=<MINIO_ROOT_USER>
#   BLOB_STORE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=<MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD>
BLOB_STORE_S3_ENDPOINT=
BLOB_STORE_S3_REGION=us-east-1
BLOB_STORE_S3_BUCKET=
BLOB_STORE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
BLOB_STORE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Envelope-encryption key provider for AI provider credentials
# (workspace-ai-credentials spec). One of: env | kms. `env` is the default
# adapter — a self-hoster is not assumed to run a cloud KMS. Startup fails
# fast when this is absent or malformed (environment-config delta).
#
# AI_KEK_KEYRING is `id:base64key[,id:base64key…]`; every key must decode
# to exactly 32 bytes (AES-256-GCM). AI_KEK_ACTIVE_ID names which key new
# credentials are wrapped under — it must be present in the keyring.
#
# The key below is an OBVIOUS PLACEHOLDER (32 repeated bytes), not a real
# secret — it is committed so a fresh clone still boots with zero
# configuration, exactly like the Mailpit and MinIO defaults above.
# Generate a real one before storing any real provider credential:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
AI_KEK_DRIVER=env
AI_KEK_KEYRING=dev:KioqKioqKioqKioqKioqKioqKioqKioqKioqKioqKio=
AI_KEK_ACTIVE_ID=dev
AI_KEK_KMS_KEY_ID=

# The variables below are read by compose.yaml, not by the zod schema in
# packages/contracts/src/env.ts — they configure the local compose stack
# itself (postgres and minio credentials) and are not part of the app's own
# configuration surface. They still belong in .env because podman/docker
# compose loads that same file automatically.

# Credentials for the postgres service in compose.yaml. Must match the
# user/password embedded in DATABASE_URL above.
# Published host ports for the compose stack. The defaults are the conventional
# ones; change them here if your machine already runs another project on them.
# `podman compose up` fails on a port collision before any service starts.
#
# That failure is the good case, because it is loud. The bad case is the one
# described above DATABASE_URL: another project already owns the port, its
# service answers, and deep-wiki connects to it. Only POSTGRES_HOST_PORT has a
# counterpart the app connects to, so it is the only one of these five that can
# fail that way — but it is also the one that holds the data.
POSTGRES_HOST_PORT=5432
MAILPIT_SMTP_HOST_PORT=1025
MAILPIT_UI_HOST_PORT=8025
MINIO_HOST_PORT=9000
MINIO_CONSOLE_HOST_PORT=9001
KROKI_HOST_PORT=8000

POSTGRES_USER=deepwiki
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=deepwiki
POSTGRES_DB=deepwiki

# Credentials for the minio service in compose.yaml (S3-compatible object
# storage console at http://localhost:9001).
MINIO_ROOT_USER=deepwiki
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=deepwiki-minio
